welcome to Nairobi National Park the  only National Park in the world the  directly borders a city if you're lucky  you'll spot wildebeest giraffes and even  the ever elusive pride of lions but  looking at the land from the air you'll  notice a different kind of creature the  standard gauge railway authorities say  out of seven possible routes this was  the best option what we established was  at all those other possible possible  routes one were going to have a lot more  impact on the park because we are going  to acquire more land from the National  Park in excess of 100 hundred and fifty  acres but what the option that we took  was the option of building a bridge  across the national we are building a  bridge wonder Anita two pyres for a  bridge of 5.8 kilometers the amount of  land that bridge will take on the  National Park is 0.1 0.5 1 of an acre  that is half an acre of land but  environmentalists accused the Kenyan  government of not following due process  before construction began well it's  really a sequence of very unfortunate  events starting off really with the the  very poorly planned and executed plans  for the narrow standard gauge railway to  Kenya at the current at the moment  really the railway is being built  through the Nairobi National Park in  contravention of court orders stopping  it and the reasons why campaigns such as  ourselves and other concerned  individuals went to court to stop it was  because we feel the right sort of  planning was not  considered or executed when thinking  about this project it floated very many  laws in Kenya particularly environmental  laws while the Train will offer  passengers a view of a lifetime while  passing through the national park  conservationists are concerned that this  construction will affect the ecosystem  and the wildlife that live here short  time surely there would be an impact  also because we've seen that they've  been blasting in the park to prove you  know to put the foundation for the  pillars for the railway etc so yes the  temporary barriers the blasting the  noise the movement of all have impact on  the wildlife in the park but again none  of that has been studied previously none  of it has been catered for in any  mitigation measures the project has been  a controversial topic in Kenya even  before construction four years ago the  first phase which runs from Kenya's  coastal city of Mombasa to Nairobi has  already been completed the second phase  is what you see here at nearly 5 million  euros a kilometre construction has cost  the country 2.8 billion euros in loans  from the Chinese government reports  suggest that more than eight million  euros has been lost in dubious land  compensation deals and with a railway  running through a National Heritage Site  many Kenyans are concerned the country  isn't getting value for money the  government says it will rehabilitate the  land once construction is finished but  for wildlife populations it may already  be too late  [Music]
 

